            </div>
            <ul class="theme-list">
                <li>
                    <strong>Anthropogenic Drivers:</strong> Human activity is the primary cause. This includes intentional burning for agriculture, resource collection (specifically <em>Madhuca longifolia</em>/Mahua flowers), and accidental negligence (cigarettes).
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Ecological Vulnerability:</strong> The Himalayan region is particularly prone due to <em>Chir Pine</em> dominance. The resin-rich needles and dry leaf litter create a highly flammable environment.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Technological Integration:</strong> There is a strong push towards modernizing detection using IoT sensors (temperature/smoke), Satellite imagery (Landsat/Sentinel), and AI-driven alert systems.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Community Role:</strong> Local communities are critical. They are often the first responders but also the primary cause of fires. Effective management requires shifting them from "causers" to "guardians" through education and alternative livelihoods.
                </li>
            </ul>
        </div>

